</p/><p><strong>Summary: </strong>
Reflecting on life and loss; on writing and sharing that writing.
</p>
        <blockquote class="quote epigraph" data-id="i-rather-believe-that-time-is-a-companion">
<p>
I rather believe that time is a companion who goes with us on the journey and
reminds us to cherish every moment because they'll never come again.  What we
leave behind is not as important as how we've lived.  After all, we're only
mortal.
</p>
<footer>&#8213; Captain Jean-Luc Picard</footer></blockquote>
<p>This year, my uncle Clifton died.  Several others who have been a fixture in my
life also died: Dolly Parton, Tim Curry, Patrick Stewart, and Walter Koenig.</p>
<p>At Clifton’s funeral, I listened to stories about a man whom I only glimpsed;
though my cousin Brett and I recognized the “make a keen observing mischevious
statement then stutter step backwards and away with a bit of shrugging chuckle.”
We saw that pantomimed by one of the eulogists, and immediately felt forgotten
memories stir.</p>
<p><time datetime="2026-10-29" title="2026-10-29">Two days ago</time>, I saw a picture of Stewart’s Gurney Halleck, and knew in my bones
that Patrick Stewart had died.  He was my Star Trek captain.  A father figure,
confounded by children, yet leading with curiosity, patience, and wisdom.</p>
<p>I started watching Star Trek the Original series when I lived in Illinois; I’d
watch reruns at Bryce’s house.  Then <em>Star Trek: the Next Generation</em> started
airing.  I watched intermittently, but it became my high school companion.</p>
<p>Then, in college, Dustin, Ryan, myself, and others would watch <em>Star Trek: the
Next Generation</em> on re-runs; once a day we’d see the episodes we missed.  In
2022, Jenny and I started a watch through of the series.  I could watch it all
again.  Though I should watch <em>Star Trek: Deep Space Nine</em> in its entirety before
I pivot back.</p>
<p>In years prior, I was more interested in <em>Star Wars</em>; which reinforced the myth of
the singular hero—though Han Solo had always been the alluring one.  Now, seeing
<em>Star Trek</em> and its nuance and massive corpus, it is more for me.  Many stories
and ethical quandries, certainly somethings are cringe (looking at you <em><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Journey%27s_End_(Star_Trek:_The_Next_Generation)">Journey’s
End</a></em>) but others are transcendent (e.g. <em><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darmok">Darmok</a></em> and <em><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Journey%27s_End_(Star_Trek:_The_Next_Generation)">The Inner Light</a></em>).</p>
<p>Death feels numb in the era of an unchecked pandemic and the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imperial_boomerang">imperial boomerang</a>,
in which the empire that I’ve been a part of now turns even more inward on
asserting hegemony.</p>
<p>Earlier this year, I finished <cite data-id="1A1FF400-CAC2-4955-ADC4-FF346E3E4D20">The Disappearance of Rituals</cite> by Byung-Chul Han, which offered a reminder that rituals are systems of
making meaning of life.</p>
<blockquote class="quote epigraph" data-id="the-loss-of-forms-of-completion-that-accompany">
<p>
The loss of forms of completion that accompany overproduction and
overconsumption leads to systemic collapses.
</p>
<footer>&#8213;Byung-Chul Han, <cite>The Disappearance of Rituals</cite></footer></blockquote>
<p>Everything flows ever one, carried away in our <em>streaming</em> culture.  I’ve been
picking up my old hand-written journals and reading them.  I can see with new
eyes the person I was then allowing to be seen.  Some obfuscation and omission
even in the privacy of my personal and private journal.</p>
<p>There are passages of “We had a great and deep talk.”  Then it moves on.  No
record of the content of those talks.  And my memory certainly struggles to
figure what it could be.  Further, I started annotating past journals (where
room allows).  Adding my perspectives or connections from decades later.</p>
<blockquote class="quote epigraph" data-id="today-time-lacks-a-solid-structure-it-is">
<p>
Today, time lacks a solid structure.  It is not a house but an erratic stream.
</p>
<footer>&#8213;Byung-Chul Han, <cite>The Disappearance of Rituals</cite></footer></blockquote>
<p>I started reading <cite data-id="76cd4eb4-5e48-4fd6-b7c2-2bffdb109ff8">The Knight</cite> by Gene Wolfe, an epistolary novel.  One in which
much is described but also much is missing, because it is a journal.  And
sometimes life happens and we fail to journal.  We’re instead busy living our
lives.</p>
<p>Reading these journals, thinking of my uncle, of the various epistolary books
I’ve read of Gene Wolfe, and of Patrick Stewart as I know him through his
characters and advocacy, I feel that sense of loss of never fully knowing
someone.  Even myself, as invariably there are gaps.</p>
<p>Life is a lossy format.</p>
<p>I have been considering moving my journaling back to pen and paper.  What one
reads on my blog is a small subset of what I actually write.  So I’d be moving
those personal things to my journal.  Maybe even drafting blog posts there.</p>
<p>But <a href="https://www.baldurbjarnason.com/2026/machine-writing-is-ash/">Writing is Magic; Machine Writing is Ash</a> by Baldur Bjarnason nudged me to
reconsider.  Or, more correctly, not write of the publishing part.  To me,
writing is very important.  I’m working at reducing my confessional tendencies,
and thus considering backing off blogging.</p>
<p>However, Baldur’s essay nudged me to reconsider.  To not cede more space to the
blood thirsty gluttons of extraction and capture.  And for my writing to be one
of what I hope to be many small lights in this world.</p>
<p>Clifton uncle died earlier this year.  Dementia ravaged his mind, yet my last
memory of him is one I’ll cherish.  We were a quarter mile from his house,
spreading the ashes of another uncle.  Clifton was confused and waiting in the
car.  He wanted to get out or move or somehow express his anxiety.</p>
<p>I walked over to the car, opened the door and started talking with him.  Asking
him about his beloved North Dakota.  He calmed and in a soft voice began telling
me about the train and the large bridge near his childhood home.  I listened,
looking back towards the house he’d lived in for as long as I could remember.</p>
<p>That home, in rural Indiana, is one place, seemingly eternal in my personal
mythology.  It is there we’d gather as a family, near the old family farm.</p>
<p>Other memories, rattling around in my brain, some having clawed their way out as
scratches upon the paper, all swirling as an eddy in my stream of life.  Ever
spiraling more into the center of who I am—a mortal man.</p>

